      "classification": "unenforced_control_claim",
      "description": "SKILL.md line 33 states that the separation between product implementation and skill authoring is 'structural here rather than asserted' because 'Skill and Task are absent from allowed-tools, so this workflow cannot invoke another skill or spawn a subagent'. The live Claude Code skills reference states the opposite: allowed-tools 'grants permission for the listed tools during the turn that invokes the skill' and 'does not restrict which tools are available: every tool remains callable, and your permission settings still govern tools that are not listed.' The field that removes tools from the pool is disallowed-tools, which this package does not use. Absence from allowed-tools therefore produces a permission prompt for an unlisted tool under default settings, not an inability to call it.\n\nThe claim is contradicted twice inside the package itself. SKILL.md line 50, added in the same port, correctly describes the unscoped Bash grant as 'an obligation this workflow keeps, not one the allowlist enforces'. references/implementation.md line 13 states that 'separately authorized delegation must preserve ownership and evidence', which presupposes delegation is possible. Line 33 and line 50 apply opposite models of the same frontmatter field.\n\nThe passage has no counterpart in the Codex parent package at src/agents/skills/dev; it was introduced by the Claude Code port, in prose that no specification governs (see the companion finding).",
      "user_impact": "A reader who trusts line 33 will believe a host-enforced boundary exists where only an instruction does. If a later instruction inside a specification or a user message asks this workflow to delegate, nothing structural stops it, and the skill has told its operator that something does. The practical risk is misplaced confidence during exactly the scenario the sentence was written to address: prompt content attempting to expand scope. The functional behavior of the skill is unaffected, which is why this is major rather than a blocker.",
      "proposed_correction": "Choose one of two dispositions, and record which. (a) Keep the boundary as an obligation and state it honestly, matching the wording already used at line 50 - for example: 'This workflow does not invoke another skill or spawn a subagent. The whole cycle runs in one session; delegation is not required to finish it. That is an obligation this workflow keeps, not one the allowlist enforces.' (b) Make the boundary real by adding 'disallowed-tools: [Skill, Task]' to the frontmatter, and describe it accurately as turn-scoped, since the restriction clears when the user sends the next message.",
      "preserved_requirements": [
        "The substantive instruction not to delegate product implementation to another skill or a subagent is correct and must survive any rewording.",
        "The resistance to later instructions that ask the workflow to delegate must survive; only the false claim about the mechanism is removed."
      ],
      "verification_cases": [
